---
title: "Rich writing"
description: "The prose surface: typography, offline math, diagrams, figures, callouts, citations, sidenotes and raw HTML passthrough."
bibliography: ../references.bib
---

Everything you'd reach for in a technical post works out of the box, rendered
server-side and shipped offline: no CDN, no client re-run on a theme toggle.

## Prose and typography

Standard Markdown works as expected: **bold**, *italic*, ~~strikethrough~~,
`inline code`, [links](https://example.com), nested lists, blockquotes, and pipe
tables with per-column alignment:

| Capability | Status | Where |
|:--|:--:|--:|
| Block model + diff | ✅ | `crates/core` |
| Live `{js}` cells | ✅ | native enhancer + vendored d3/Plot |
| PDF / non-HTML output | ❌ | out of scope |

> Smart typography turns straight quotes into curly ones and handles ellipses…,
> matching the look of a hand-written post.

Code blocks are syntax-highlighted **server-side** (in Rust, via syntect) and get
a hover **Copy** button. Highlighting ships fully offline: the colours are emitted
as scope classes the theme styles, so the exported HTML needs no CDN, the first
paint is already coloured, and the light/dark toggle restyles code with nothing to
re-run.

A fence whose language token isn't recognised still renders, just unhighlighted.
That is easy to mistake for broken highlighting, so `taliesin check` reports it:
` ```pyton ` gets a located "unknown code language" warning. When a block is meant
to be plain (a terminal transcript, sample output), label it `text` or `console`
and it stays plain without a warning.

An info string may carry **attributes after the language** in the form other tools use
(` ```rust,ignore `, ` ```python should_panic `, ` ```rust,no_run,edition2021 `). Taliesin
reads the language from the first token and ignores the rest, so code pasted from an
mdBook, rustdoc, Pandoc, Docusaurus or GitHub project highlights as it did there instead of
losing its colours. Nothing acts on the attributes: they are another tool's instructions,
kept so the source stays portable in both directions.

```rust
/// Every rendered block carries a content-hash id and its source range.
struct Block {
    id: String,        // stable across edits while the content is unchanged
    sourcepos: String, // "startLine:startCol-endLine:endCol"
    html: String,
}
```

## Math

Math renders server-side with KaTeX and ships fully offline (the fonts are inlined
at build time). Inline math like $e^{i\pi} + 1 = 0$ sits in running text; display
math stands alone; and a labelled equation is **numbered and cross-referencable**.
The discrete Fourier transform [@cooley1965] is given by @eq-dft:

$$
X[k] = \sum_{n=0}^{N-1} x[n]\, e^{-2\pi i k n / N}
$$ {#eq-dft}

Multi-line aligned environments work too:

$$
\begin{aligned}
  \nabla \cdot \mathbf{E} &= \rho/\varepsilon_0 \\
  \nabla \times \mathbf{B} &= \mu_0\mathbf{J} + \mu_0\varepsilon_0 \frac{\partial \mathbf{E}}{\partial t}
\end{aligned}
$$

## Diagrams

A `{mermaid}` code block renders client-side with mermaid.js, loaded lazily the
first time a diagram appears.
Diagrams-from-code is a first-class feature, not an afterthought; the colours are
CSS-driven, so they follow the theme. Adding `%%| label: fig-…` and `%%| fig-cap:`
turns a diagram into a numbered, captioned figure you can cross-reference, just
like an image. As @fig-render shows, one block model feeds three render targets:

```{mermaid}
%%| label: fig-render
%%| fig-cap: "One block model, three render targets."
flowchart LR
  Q[".tmd"] --> BM["block model"]
  BM --> H["HTML page"]
  BM --> R["slide deck"]
  BM --> K["book + TOC"]
```

Every mermaid diagram type works (**sequence**, **state**, class, ER, gantt,
and more), so you can document a protocol or a state machine from a few lines of
text. Here is the live-edit loop as a sequence diagram:

```{mermaid}
sequenceDiagram
  autonumber
  participant E as Editor
  participant S as Server
  participant P as Preview
  E->>S: save .tmd
  S->>S: re-parse + diff by block id
  S-->>P: only the changed blocks
  P->>P: swap them in (scroll kept)
  Note over P: live canvases keep their state
```

…and the warm kernel's lifecycle as a state diagram:

```{mermaid}
stateDiagram-v2
  [*] --> Idle
  Idle --> Running: run changed cell(s)
  Running --> Idle: outputs spliced back
  Running --> Interrupted: exceeds timeout
  Interrupted --> Idle
  Idle --> Respawned: process died
  Respawned --> Idle: re-run all cells
```

## Figures and captions

A standalone image with a `{#fig-…}` attribute, or a code cell tagged with
`#| label: fig-…` and `#| fig-cap:`, becomes a numbered `<figure>`, and `@fig-`
cross-references resolve to its number. @fig-scree is drawn by a matplotlib cell
that sets *no* theme colours:
inline figures default to a transparent background with neutral-grey axes, so they
track the light/dark theme on their own.

```{python}
#| label: fig-scree
#| fig-cap: "Variance explained per principal component."
#| echo: false
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

components = range(1, 8)
explained = [42, 26, 15, 8.5, 5, 2.5, 1]

fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(6, 3.4), tight_layout=True)
ax.bar(components, explained, width=0.6, color="#4c8dff", alpha=0.85)
ax.plot(
    components, explained,
    color="#e0566b", linewidth=2, linestyle="--", marker="o", markersize=4,
)
ax.annotate(
    "elbow", xy=(3, 15), xytext=(3.6, 27),
    color="#e0566b", fontsize=11,
    arrowprops=dict(arrowstyle="->", color="#e0566b"),
)
ax.set_xlabel("Principal component")
ax.set_ylabel("Variance explained (%)")
ax.set_xticks(list(components))
for spine in ("top", "right"):
    ax.spines[spine].set_visible(False)
plt.show()
```

### What an image gets without asking

Point at a `.png`, `.jpg` or `.gif` and the rest is automatic. There is nothing to
configure and nothing to pre-process.

**Its box is reserved before it loads.** Every local raster image is emitted with the
intrinsic `width` and `height` read from the file itself, so the prose below it never
jumps as the image arrives. This happens in the preview too, which is the point: a
preview that does not shift the way the built page shifts is not previewing it.

**Your bytes ship as you wrote them.** The build copies each image across unchanged; it
derives no smaller copies and re-encodes nothing, so what a reader downloads is the file
you pointed at. If that matters for a page, shrink or re-encode the source yourself before
you reference it — `cwebp`, `avifenc` or an export dialogue all do it better than a
document tool guessing on your behalf, and the result is a file you can look at.

**The first image stays eager.** Later images get `loading="lazy"`, but the first one on
the page does not: it is the most likely largest-contentful-paint element, and deferring
it would delay the very thing lazy-loading is meant to help.

An `.svg` is the one exception to the box-reservation rule above: it has no intrinsic
pixel size to state, so nothing is emitted for it.

## Callouts

Fenced divs become callouts, layout grids, or generic containers.

::: {.callout-tip}
## Tip

Use a `title=` attribute or a leading heading to label a callout; otherwise it
falls back to the callout kind.
:::

A callout with `collapse="true"` becomes a native, JavaScript-free disclosure:

::: {.callout-note collapse="true"}
## The full block model (click to expand)

Every top-level element carries `data-block-id` (a content hash), `data-sourcepos`
(its source range), and, for included files, `data-source-file`.
:::

## Citations and cross-references

With a `bibliography:` in the front matter, `[@key]` citations become numbered
links to an auto-generated References section, formatted from the BibTeX file.
This page cites the *TeXbook* [@knuth1984] and the FFT paper [@cooley1965], and
groups with locators work too: [@knuth1984; @cooley1965, p. 297]. Cross-references
resolve to labelled links across `@fig-`, `@sec-`, `@tbl-`, `@eq-`, `@lst-`,
`@thm-`, and `@def-`, carrying the resolved number when known (as `@eq-dft` and
`@fig-scree` above do): **across chapters**, so a `@fig-` in one chapter resolves
to its figure in another.

In a site or book, `bibliography:` can instead live once in `_site.yml` and be shared
by every page; a page's own `bibliography:` is then merged over it. See
[Configuration](../reference/configuration.tmd).

## Footnotes and sidenotes

Footnotes use the standard `[^label]` syntax. A reference renders as a superscript
number, and the note itself renders **in the right margin, beside the line that cites
it**.[^demo] There is no list of endnotes at the bottom of the page: a note sits where
you need it, so reading one costs a glance instead of a jump to the end and back.

[^demo]: Like this one. Write `[^label]` where you want the marker, and `[^label]: …`
    anywhere for the body; Taliesin does the numbering and the placement.

Narrow the window past the point where there is a margin to put a note in and the notes
fold away; tapping a number reveals that one in place. Print does the same, since paper
has no reader to tap. This is the default, not a setting: it is the placement a screen
makes possible and paper never did, and reaching for a `footnotes:` knob to get it would
be a worse default hiding behind a configuration key.

Notes are for asides short enough to sit in a narrow column, and a definition carrying
block content (a list, a quote, a code block) is flattened to its text with a warning,
because a margin note can only hold inline content.

For an aside with no reference to hang it off, a `::: {.column-margin}` block floats
into the same margin column on a wide screen and folds into the flow on a narrow one:
web-native marginalia a PDF can't do. `.column-margin` is the only spelling — it used to
also accept `.sidenote`, `.marginnote`, and `.aside` as aliases (Tufte/Distill/Quarto
muscle memory), retired on 2026-08-03, since a single tool gains nothing from four names
for the same box. Footnotes and margin blocks share one column, so the two never
collide.

::: {.column-margin}
This is a margin note. On a wide screen it sits in the margin, aligned to where it was
written; narrow the window and it collapses to an indented note. Math works here too:
$\sigma^2 = \operatorname{Var}(X)$.
:::

The body keeps flowing in the reading column while the margin note sits alongside the
paragraph it annotates, so a digression never interrupts the main thread.

The opposite move on the same axis is to make a block *wider* than the reading column.
`::: {.column-page}` widens up to the page width, and `::: {.column-screen}` goes edge
to edge. Both are for content the ~70-character measure is simply wrong for: a wide
table, a multi-panel plot, a diagram whose labels only fit at size.

::: {.column-page}
| Escape | Width | Use it for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| *(none)* | the reading measure | prose, which is what the measure is tuned for |
| `.column-page` | up to the page width | a wide table, a figure that needs the room |
| `.column-screen` | edge to edge | a full-bleed band |
:::

Neither is a new block type — they are plain classes, so they work on a table, a
figure, a listing, or a bare div, and the block keeps its identity (a `@fig-`
cross-reference still resolves through one). On a page that shows a table-of-contents
rail an escape grows leftward instead of centring, keeping its right edge flush with
the text, because the rail is real content and running underneath it would put text
over text.

## Raw HTML passthrough

A `{=html}` block is emitted verbatim, so you can drop in markup the Markdown
grammar doesn't cover (custom widgets, embeds, audio players):

```{=html}
<div style="display:flex;gap:.6rem;align-items:center;padding:.7rem 1rem;border-radius:10px;
            color:var(--tali-fg);border:1px solid var(--tali-border);
            background:linear-gradient(90deg,rgba(99,102,241,.16),rgba(236,72,153,.16));
            font:600 14px ui-sans-serif,system-ui">
  <span style="font-size:1.5rem">🔌</span>
  <span>This badge is raw HTML, passed through by a <code>{=html}</code> block, not escaped. Its
        inline styles use the page's theme variables, so it tracks light/dark too.</span>
</div>
```

## Editor gestures: paste, drop, and rename

The VS Code companion turns the fiddly parts of authoring into gestures. None of them is a
new syntax to learn: each one writes the same Markdown you would have typed, and the
companion asks the language server what to write, so a pasted table and a table you format
by hand come out byte-identical.

**Paste an image** from the clipboard and the file lands beside the document as
`<document>-01.png`, with a figure block inserted and the cursor waiting in the caption.
Tab moves to the `#fig-` label, so the two things only you can write are the two things
you are asked for:

```
![▮caption▮](bayes-01.png){#fig-▮label▮}
```

**Drag an image in from the Explorer** to get a path relative to the document. If the file
sits outside the document's folder, the companion says so before inserting it and offers to
copy it in, because the build cannot bundle an asset it cannot reach: that is the same rule,
from the same code, that makes `build` warn about a stray asset.

**Paste a spreadsheet** (or any HTML table, from a browser or a spreadsheet app) and it
becomes an aligned pipe table. Tab-separated plain text can become one too, but it is
offered in the paste-as menu rather than done automatically: text with tabs in it is
usually just text, and silently turning it into a table is worse than one extra keystroke.

**Paste a URL over a selection** to wrap it: select `manual`, paste
`https://taliesin.sh/guide`, get `[manual](https://taliesin.sh/guide)`.

**Paste a BibTeX entry** and it is appended to the bibliography your front matter names,
with `[@key]` inserted at the cursor. Both edits are one undo. If the key is already in the
`.bib` you get the citation and no duplicate entry, since a duplicate key is something
`check` would report.

**Drop any file** from the Explorer to get a reference to it, plus a verdict on whether
the build will be able to ship it: a file outside the document's folder is one the build
would drop, so you are offered a copy rather than a broken link.

**Rename or move a `.tmd`** and every reference is repaired: the includes, embeds, links
and `_site.yml` entries that point *at* it, and, when it changes folder, its own images and
includes that point *out*. It is one undo, so if the result is not what you wanted, Ctrl+Z
puts the rename and the repair back together. Renaming an image repairs the figures that
show it, the same way.

Two limits worth knowing, so they do not read as bugs:

- **A document in a folder with no `_site.yml` gets no inbound repair.** Without a project
  file there is no declared boundary, and guessing one (walking up to the nearest `.git`,
  say) would rewrite files in a tree you never said was yours. Its own outgoing references
  are still rebased.
- **Pasting a BibTeX entry into a document with no bibliography** inserts the citation and
  stops. It does not create a `.bib` and edit your front matter to point at it; the
  existing "citations but no bibliography" diagnostic tells you what is missing.

Finally, a `page.tmd:12:` location in the preview or `check` output is **clickable**, so a
diagnostic in the terminal takes you to the line.
